# Local command preauthorization migrated from Claude permissions.
# Sources: ~/.claude/settings.json and this project's .claude/settings.json.
# No sandbox, approval-mode, MCP, or harness-hook changes were made by the porting run -- that is a
# statement about what was PORTED, not about what the harness HAS: this harness ships a hook layer
# (PreToolUse, PermissionRequest, PostToolUse, SessionStart and a command handler type), and the
# whole-command-string guard that backs the Claude floor was simply never written for it. The ceiling
# below is therefore a SCOPE decision, not a structural bound.
# Literal argv prefixes only: this is NOT equivalent to the Claude permission guard. Matching reads
# argv[0] onwards, so a forbidden act reached through an exec wrapper (xargs, env, a script) never
# appears as the command being matched.
# Shell/URL globs, file-edit permissions and historical local approvals are not copied.
# NO ABSOLUTE PATH APPEARS HERE, and that costs coverage rather than nothing: every git/terraform
# deny is carried in four spellings (bare, `-C .`, `-C ../<sibling>` for each repo), so the ACT stays
# forbidden -- but an invocation that types a machine-absolute path matches none of them. The Claude
# guard reads the whole string and does not have that gap. Do not close it by pasting a path back in.
# Hook-script allows are repo-relative literals, the same form this file already uses for
# `git -C .` and `terraform -chdir=iac`. Whether the matcher compares a relative token
# literally or canonicalises it first is NOT measured; either way the failure is a permission
# prompt and never a hole, because every one of them is an allow.
# ~~A script added or deleted needs this list updated, and `hooks/scripts/codex-rules.test.sh` is
# what reddens if it is not.~~ STRUCK 2026-09-10 -- TRUE of DELETED, FALSE of ADDED, and struck
# rather than corrected because it is a claim about a control, in the control's own file, and
# whoever read it took the sync for mechanical. Arm 4 was rule->disk only: it catches a rule
# outliving its file and sees nothing at all when a file arrives naming no rule. Measured at the
# head that struck it, THREE tracked scripts were missing from this list with the suite green at
# 8 passed, 0 failed -- `cadence-notice.sh`, `cadence-notice.test.sh`, and `codex-rules.test.sh`,
# the last of them the very gate the struck sentence named.
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW is two arms rather than one: `arm 4` asserts rule->disk (every path named here
# resolves) and `arm 4b` asserts disk->rule (every tracked `*.sh` under `hooks/scripts/` is named
# here). Each refuses to pass over an empty set rather than printing a zero as a green.
# THE SCOPE IS THAT DIRECTORY AND NOT THE TREE, deliberately: `scripts/milestone-create.sh` is
# tracked, is run with `bash`, and is named here by NOTHING on purpose -- its absence from every
# allow layer is what leaves a permission prompt standing where the deleted rule 11 milestone
# verification used to be. Do not add an allow for it to quiet a gate; no gate asks for one.
# More restrictive matching rules win. Commands remain subject to AGENTS.md.
prefix_rule(pattern=["actionlint"], decision="allow")
prefix_rule(pattern=["awk"], decision="allow")
prefix_rule(pattern=["basename"], decision="allow")
